# Expectation record for 09_optimization_levels/005_inlining_invariance.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 005_inlining_invariance
area               = 09_optimization_levels
description        = Small-function results unchanged whether inlined or called
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every argument is read from volatile storage, so no input is known at compile time and each
result is computed from values observed at run time.  All arithmetic stays inside the range
of int, including the bounded factorial, so no signed overflow can occur whether or not a
call is inlined.  No object is read before assignment.  No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
add3=24
scale=36
clamp_low=10
clamp_mid=15
clamp_high=20
accumulate=28
factorial=5040
combine=21
nested_calls=70
END
